WEBVTT

00:00.650 --> 00:02.410
I've draw an arbitrary
function here.

00:02.410 --> 00:03.785
And what we're
going to try to do

00:03.785 --> 00:06.150
is approximate this
arbitrary function--

00:06.150 --> 00:08.530
we don't know what it
is-- using a polynomial.

00:08.530 --> 00:10.329
We'll keep adding terms
to that polynomial.

00:10.329 --> 00:11.870
But to do this,
we're going to assume

00:11.870 --> 00:14.510
that we can evaluate
the function at 0,

00:14.510 --> 00:16.210
that it gives us some
value, and that we

00:16.210 --> 00:19.420
can keep taking the
derivative of the function

00:19.420 --> 00:21.900
and evaluating the
first, the second,

00:21.900 --> 00:25.450
and the third derivative, so
on and so forth, at 0 as well.

00:25.450 --> 00:28.940
So we're assuming that
we know what f of 0 is.

00:28.940 --> 00:31.720
We're assuming that we
know what f prime of 0 is.

00:31.720 --> 00:34.450
We're assuming that we know
the second derivative at 0.

00:34.450 --> 00:38.340
We're assuming that we know
the third derivative at 0.

00:38.340 --> 00:40.300
So maybe I'll write
it-- third derivative.

00:40.300 --> 00:44.940
I'll just write f prime prime
at 0, and so forth and so on.

00:44.940 --> 00:47.910
So let's think about how we
can approximate this using

00:47.910 --> 00:50.770
polynomials of ever
increasing length.

00:50.770 --> 00:53.499
So we could have a
polynomial of just one term.

00:53.499 --> 00:55.040
And it would just
be a constant term.

00:55.040 --> 00:58.050
So this would be a
polynomial of degree 0.

00:58.050 --> 00:59.930
And if we have a constant
term, we at least

00:59.930 --> 01:03.580
might want to make that constant
polynomial-- it really is just

01:03.580 --> 01:08.160
a constant function-- equal
the function at f of 0.

01:08.160 --> 01:12.220
So at first, maybe
we just want p of 0,

01:12.220 --> 01:15.930
where p is the polynomial
that we're going to construct,

01:15.930 --> 01:20.060
we want p of 0 to
be equal to f of 0.

01:20.060 --> 01:22.970
So if we want to do that
using a polynomial of only one

01:22.970 --> 01:26.080
term, of only one
constant term, we

01:26.080 --> 01:30.250
can just set p of x
is equal to f of 0.

01:30.250 --> 01:33.000
So if I were to graph it,
it would look like this.

01:33.000 --> 01:37.260
It would just be a
horizontal line at f of 0.

01:37.260 --> 01:40.080
And you could say, Sal, that's
a horrible approximation.

01:40.080 --> 01:42.360
It only approximates the
function at this point.

01:42.360 --> 01:44.526
Looks like we got lucky at
a couple of other points,

01:44.526 --> 01:46.170
but it's really bad
everywhere else.

01:46.170 --> 01:47.628
And now I would
tell you, well, try

01:47.628 --> 01:49.660
to do any better using
a horizontal line.

01:49.660 --> 01:52.520
At least we got it
right at f of 0.

01:52.520 --> 01:56.345
So this is about as good as we
can do with just a constant.

01:56.345 --> 01:58.220
And even though-- I just
want to remind you--

01:58.220 --> 01:59.720
this might not look
like a constant,

01:59.720 --> 02:02.080
but we're assuming that
given the function,

02:02.080 --> 02:03.720
we could evaluate
it at 0 and that

02:03.720 --> 02:05.020
will just give us a number.

02:05.020 --> 02:07.800
So whatever number that was, we
would put it right over here.

02:07.800 --> 02:10.020
We'd say p of x is
equal to that number.

02:10.020 --> 02:15.080
It would just be a horizontal
line right there at f of 0.

02:15.080 --> 02:17.120
But that obviously
is not so great.

02:17.120 --> 02:18.900
So let's add some
more constraints.

02:18.900 --> 02:22.280
Beyond the fact that we want
p of 0 to be equal to f of 0,

02:22.280 --> 02:27.730
let's say that we
also want p prime at 0

02:27.730 --> 02:31.330
to be the same thing
as f prime at 0.

02:31.330 --> 02:33.590
Let me do this in a new color.

02:33.590 --> 02:36.070
So we also want,
in the new color,

02:36.070 --> 02:38.930
we also want-- that's
not a new color.

02:38.930 --> 02:41.360
We also want p prime.

02:41.360 --> 02:44.030
We want the first derivative
of our polynomial,

02:44.030 --> 02:46.880
when evaluated at 0,
to be the same thing

02:46.880 --> 02:50.650
as the first derivative of the
function when evaluated at 0.

02:50.650 --> 02:53.950
And we don't want to lose
this right over here.

02:53.950 --> 03:00.380
So what if we set p of x
as being equal to f of 0?

03:00.380 --> 03:02.270
So we're taking our old
p of x, but now we're

03:02.270 --> 03:05.000
going to add another term so
that the derivatives match up.

03:05.000 --> 03:11.150
Plus f prime of times x.

03:11.150 --> 03:13.030
So let's think about
this a little bit.

03:13.030 --> 03:16.780
If we use this as our new
polynomial, what happens?

03:16.780 --> 03:18.290
What is p is 0?

03:18.290 --> 03:21.690
p of 0 is going to
be equal to-- you're

03:21.690 --> 03:27.180
going to have f of 0 plus
whatever this f prime of 0

03:27.180 --> 03:28.850
is times 0.

03:28.850 --> 03:32.210
If you put a 0 in for x, this
term is just going to be 0.

03:32.210 --> 03:35.380
So you're going to be left
with p of 0 is equal to f of 0.

03:35.380 --> 03:36.340
That's cool.

03:36.340 --> 03:38.560
That's just as good
as our first version.

03:38.560 --> 03:40.390
Now what's the
derivative over here?

03:40.390 --> 03:45.374
So the derivative is p
prime of x is equal to-- you

03:45.374 --> 03:46.540
take the derivative of this.

03:46.540 --> 03:49.910
This is just a constant,
so its derivative is 0.

03:49.910 --> 03:52.110
The derivative of a
coefficient times x

03:52.110 --> 03:53.950
is just going to
be the coefficient.

03:53.950 --> 03:56.480
So it's going to
be f prime of 0.

03:56.480 --> 04:02.800
So if you evaluate it
at 0-- so p prime of 0.

04:02.800 --> 04:04.972
Or the derivative of
our polynomial evaluated

04:04.972 --> 04:07.430
at 0-- I know it's a little
weird because we're not using--

04:07.430 --> 04:10.261
we're doing a p prime of x
of f of 0 and all of this.

04:10.261 --> 04:12.760
But just remember, what's the
variable, what's the constant,

04:12.760 --> 04:14.390
and hopefully, it'll make sense.

04:14.390 --> 04:17.500
So this is just obviously
going to be f prime of 0.

04:17.500 --> 04:19.270
Its derivative is
a constant value.

04:19.270 --> 04:20.769
This is a constant
value right here.

04:20.769 --> 04:23.352
We're assuming that we can take
the derivative of our function

04:23.352 --> 04:27.210
and evaluate that thing at
0 to give a constant value.

04:27.210 --> 04:30.140
So if p prime of x is equal
to this constant value,

04:30.140 --> 04:33.420
obviously, p prime
of x evaluated at 0

04:33.420 --> 04:35.160
is going to be that value.

04:35.160 --> 04:39.390
But what's cool about
this right here,

04:39.390 --> 04:42.650
this polynomial that has a 0
degree term and a first degree

04:42.650 --> 04:48.550
term, is now this polynomial
is equal to our function at x

04:48.550 --> 04:49.730
is equal to 0.

04:49.730 --> 04:52.270
And it also has the
same first derivative.

04:52.270 --> 04:56.640
It also has the same
slope at x is equal to 0.

04:56.640 --> 04:59.824
So this thing will look,
this new polynomial

04:59.824 --> 05:01.740
with two terms-- getting
a little bit better--

05:01.740 --> 05:04.400
it will look
something like that.

05:04.400 --> 05:08.560
It will essentially have--
it'll look like a tangent line

05:08.560 --> 05:13.050
at f of 0, at x is equal to 0.

05:13.050 --> 05:17.860
So we're doing better, but still
not a super good approximation.

05:17.860 --> 05:20.510
It kind of is going in
the same general direction

05:20.510 --> 05:22.462
as our function around 0.

05:22.462 --> 05:24.170
But maybe we can do
better by making sure

05:24.170 --> 05:26.590
that they have the
same second derivative.

05:26.590 --> 05:29.480
And to try to have the same
second derivative while still

05:29.480 --> 05:33.480
having the same first derivative
and the same value at 0,

05:33.480 --> 05:38.170
let's try to do
something interesting.

05:38.170 --> 05:40.350
Let's define p of x.

05:40.350 --> 05:41.780
So let's make it clear.

05:41.780 --> 05:43.970
This was our first try.

05:43.970 --> 05:46.990
This is our second
try right over here.

05:46.990 --> 05:49.740
And I'm about to embark
on our third try.

05:49.740 --> 05:53.050
So in our third try, my goal is
that the value of my polynomial

05:53.050 --> 05:55.740
is the same as the value
of the function at 0.

05:55.740 --> 05:57.590
They have the same
derivative at 0.

05:57.590 --> 06:00.460
And they also have the same
second derivative at 0.

06:00.460 --> 06:03.280
So let's define my
polynomial to be

06:03.280 --> 06:05.290
equal to-- so I'm going
to do the first two

06:05.290 --> 06:07.560
terms of these guys
right over here.

06:07.560 --> 06:13.490
So it's going to be
f of 0 plus f prime

06:13.490 --> 06:17.280
of 0 times x, so exactly
what we did here.

06:17.280 --> 06:19.040
But now let me add another term.

06:19.040 --> 06:22.930
I'll do the other
term in a new color.

06:22.930 --> 06:24.560
And I'm going to
put a 1/2 out here.

06:24.560 --> 06:27.210
And hopefully it might make
sense why I'm about to do this.

06:27.210 --> 06:31.150
Plus 1/2 times the
second derivative

06:31.150 --> 06:36.150
of our function
evaluated at 0 x squared.

06:36.150 --> 06:38.090
And when we evaluate
the derivative of this,

06:38.090 --> 06:40.890
I think you'll see
why this 1/2 is there.

06:40.890 --> 06:46.170
Because now let's evaluate
this and its derivatives at 0.

06:46.170 --> 06:51.700
So if we evaluate p of 0, p of
0 is going to be equal to what?

06:51.700 --> 06:53.350
Well, you have
this constant term.

06:53.350 --> 06:55.770
If you evaluate it at 0,
this x and this x squared

06:55.770 --> 06:56.800
are both going to be 0.

06:56.800 --> 06:58.660
So those terms are
going to go away.

06:58.660 --> 07:01.860
So p of 0 is still
equal to f of 0.

07:01.860 --> 07:04.900
If you take the derivative
of p of x-- so let

07:04.900 --> 07:06.770
me take the
derivative right here.

07:06.770 --> 07:08.130
I'll do it in yellow.

07:08.130 --> 07:10.180
So the derivative
of my new p of x

07:10.180 --> 07:12.936
is going to be equal to-- so
this term is going to go away.

07:12.936 --> 07:13.810
It's a constant term.

07:13.810 --> 07:17.320
It's going to be
equal to f prime of 0.

07:17.320 --> 07:18.840
That's the coefficient on this.

07:18.840 --> 07:24.100
Plus-- this is the power
rule right here-- 2 times 1/2

07:24.100 --> 07:30.430
is just 1, plus f prime
prime of 0 times x.

07:30.430 --> 07:32.120
Take the 2, multiply
it times 1/2,

07:32.120 --> 07:34.620
and decrement that
2 right there.

07:34.620 --> 07:39.540
I think you now have a sense
of why we put the 1/2 there.

07:39.540 --> 07:42.590
It's making it so that we don't
end up with the 2 coefficient

07:42.590 --> 07:43.990
out front.

07:43.990 --> 07:46.630
Now what is p prime of 0?

07:46.630 --> 07:50.770
So let me write it right
here. p prime of 0 is what?

07:50.770 --> 07:53.020
Well, this term right here
is just going to be 0,

07:53.020 --> 07:55.780
so you're left with this
constant value right over here.

07:55.780 --> 07:58.310
So it's going to
be f prime of 0.

07:58.310 --> 08:01.150
So so far, our third
generation polynomial

08:01.150 --> 08:03.300
has all the properties
of the first two.

08:03.300 --> 08:07.940
And let's see how it does
on its third derivative,

08:07.940 --> 08:09.930
or I should say the
second derivative.

08:09.930 --> 08:13.370
So p prime prime of
x is equal to-- this

08:13.370 --> 08:15.566
is a constant, so
its derivative is 0.

08:15.566 --> 08:17.690
So you just take the
coefficient on the second term

08:17.690 --> 08:20.940
is equal to f prime prime of 0.

08:20.940 --> 08:24.840
So what's the second
derivative of p evaluated at 0?

08:24.840 --> 08:27.010
Well, it's just going to
be this constant value.

08:27.010 --> 08:30.120
It's going to be f
prime prime of 0.

08:30.120 --> 08:32.919
So notice, by adding
this term, now,

08:32.919 --> 08:38.150
not only is our polynomial value
the same thing as our function

08:38.150 --> 08:41.010
value at 0, its derivative
at 0 is the same thing

08:41.010 --> 08:42.711
as the derivative of
the function at 0.

08:42.711 --> 08:44.710
And its second derivative
at 0 is the same thing

08:44.710 --> 08:47.399
as the second derivative
of the function at 0.

08:47.399 --> 08:48.940
So we're getting
pretty good at this.

08:48.940 --> 08:51.350
And you might guess that
there's a pattern here.

08:51.350 --> 08:56.290
Every term we add, it'll allow
us to set up the situation so

08:56.290 --> 09:01.060
that the n-th derivative
of our approximation at 0

09:01.060 --> 09:03.080
will be the same thing
as the n-th derivative

09:03.080 --> 09:05.120
of our function at 0.

09:05.120 --> 09:07.200
So in general, if we
wanted to keep doing this,

09:07.200 --> 09:08.700
if we had a lot of
time on our hands

09:08.700 --> 09:12.540
and we wanted to just keep
adding terms to our polynomial,

09:12.540 --> 09:14.490
we could-- and let me
do this in a new color.

09:14.490 --> 09:16.330
Maybe I'll do it in a
color I already used.

09:16.330 --> 09:18.185
We could make our
polynomial approximation.

09:21.610 --> 09:24.830
So the first term, the constant
term, will just be f of 0.

09:24.830 --> 09:29.830
Then the next term will
be f prime of 0 times x.

09:29.830 --> 09:33.650
Then the next term
will be f prime

09:33.650 --> 09:38.440
prime of 0 times
1/2 times x squared.

09:38.440 --> 09:40.829
I just rewrote that in a
slightly different order.

09:40.829 --> 09:43.370
Then the next term, if we want
to make their third derivative

09:43.370 --> 09:49.340
the same at 0, would be
f prime prime prime of 0.

09:49.340 --> 09:50.840
The third derivative
of the function

09:50.840 --> 09:59.280
at 0, times 1/2 times 1/3,
so 1 over 2 times 3 times x

09:59.280 --> 10:00.490
to the third.

10:00.490 --> 10:01.640
And we can keep going.

10:01.640 --> 10:03.880
Maybe you you'll start
to see a pattern here.

10:03.880 --> 10:06.630
Plus, if we want to make
their fourth derivatives

10:06.630 --> 10:10.854
at 0 coincide, it would
be the fourth derivative

10:10.854 --> 10:11.520
of the function.

10:11.520 --> 10:13.390
I could put a 4 up
there, but this is really

10:13.390 --> 10:18.320
emphasizing-- it's the fourth
derivative at 0 times 1 over--

10:18.320 --> 10:19.550
and I'll change the order.

10:19.550 --> 10:21.300
Instead of writing it
in increasing order,

10:21.300 --> 10:28.710
I'll write it as 4 times 3
times 2 times x to the fourth.

10:28.710 --> 10:30.200
And you can verify
it for yourself.

10:30.200 --> 10:31.620
If we just had this
only, and if you

10:31.620 --> 10:33.420
were to take the fourth
derivative of this,

10:33.420 --> 10:35.800
evaluate it at 0,
it'll be the same thing

10:35.800 --> 10:39.630
as the fourth derivative of
the function evaluated at 0.

10:39.630 --> 10:42.150
And in general, you
can keep adding terms

10:42.150 --> 10:45.210
where the n-th term
will look like this.

10:45.210 --> 10:50.890
The n-th derivative of your
function evaluated at 0 times

10:50.890 --> 10:56.180
x to the n over n factorial.

10:56.180 --> 10:59.380
Notice this is the same
thing as 4 factorial.

10:59.380 --> 11:03.081
4 factorial is equal to 4
times 3 times 2 times 1.

11:03.081 --> 11:04.580
You don't have to
write the 1 there,

11:04.580 --> 11:06.300
but you could put it there.

11:06.300 --> 11:10.300
This right here is the same
thing as 3 factorial-- 3 times

11:10.300 --> 11:12.300
2 times 1.

11:12.300 --> 11:13.610
I didn't put the 1 there.

11:13.610 --> 11:16.650
This right here is the same
thing as 2 factorial, 2 times

11:16.650 --> 11:17.530
1.

11:17.530 --> 11:18.570
This is the same thing.

11:18.570 --> 11:20.694
We didn't write anything,
but you could divide this

11:20.694 --> 11:23.840
by 1 factorial, which
is the same thing as 1.

11:23.840 --> 11:25.650
And you can divide
this by 0 factorial,

11:25.650 --> 11:26.880
which also happens to be 1.

11:26.880 --> 11:29.120
We won't have to study
it too much over here.

11:29.120 --> 11:32.730
But this general series that
I've kind of set up right here

11:32.730 --> 11:34.660
is called the Maclaurin series.

11:37.530 --> 11:39.800
And you can approximate
a polynomial.

11:39.800 --> 11:42.290
And we'll see it leads to
some pretty powerful results

11:42.290 --> 11:43.280
later on.

11:43.280 --> 11:45.610
But what happens-- and I
don't have the computing power

11:45.610 --> 11:47.310
in my brain to draw
the graph properly--

11:47.310 --> 11:49.720
is that when only
the functions equal,

11:49.720 --> 11:51.120
you get that horizontal line.

11:54.480 --> 11:56.430
When you make the
function equal 0

11:56.430 --> 11:59.470
and their first
derivatives equal at 0,

11:59.470 --> 12:01.940
then you have something that
looks like the tangent line.

12:01.940 --> 12:03.850
When you add another
degree, it might

12:03.850 --> 12:06.490
approximate the polynomial
something like this.

12:06.490 --> 12:11.170
When you add another degree, it
might look something like that.

12:11.170 --> 12:13.860
And as you keep adding
more and more degrees, when

12:13.860 --> 12:15.360
you keep adding
more and more terms,

12:15.360 --> 12:17.250
it gets closer
and closer around,

12:17.250 --> 12:20.490
especially as you get
close to x is equal to 0.

12:20.490 --> 12:24.130
But in theory, if you add
an infinite number of terms,

12:24.130 --> 12:26.660
you shouldn't be able to do--
I haven't proven this to you,

12:26.660 --> 12:27.910
so that's why I'm saying that.

12:27.910 --> 12:29.590
I haven't proved it yet to you.

12:29.590 --> 12:31.600
But if you add an
infinite number of terms,

12:31.600 --> 12:33.870
all of the derivatives
should be the same.

12:33.870 --> 12:36.900
And then the function
should pretty much

12:36.900 --> 12:38.600
look like each other.

12:38.600 --> 12:41.880
In the next video, I'll do
this with some actual functions

12:41.880 --> 12:44.390
just so it makes a
little bit more sense.

12:44.390 --> 12:46.660
And just so you know,
the Maclaurin series

12:46.660 --> 12:48.920
is a special case
of the Taylor series

12:48.920 --> 12:50.657
because we're centering it at 0.

12:50.657 --> 12:52.240
And when you're doing
a Taylor series,

12:52.240 --> 12:53.900
you can pick any center point.

12:53.900 --> 12:58.710
We'll focus on the
Maclaurin right now.
